\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The exact sign response on the two-by-two torus}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{Use the [orbit gauge–Higgs weight](fdip-010A) of the sign torus
in \ref{fdip-010H}, with external factor #{\ue^{\eta u\phi_x}} at
one site #{x} and unit additional factors elsewhere. The two signs
have equal zero-field total weight #{550656/2}, since their scalar
marginal is uniform by \ref{fdip-010Q}. Thus
##{\begin{aligned}
 Z_\eta&=550656\cosh(\eta u),\\
 \langle\phi_x\rangle_\eta
 &=\frac{\ue^{\eta u}-\ue^{-\eta u}}{\ue^{\eta u}+\ue^{-\eta u}}
 =\tanh(\eta u).
\end{aligned}}
The same single-site mean holds at every volume with sign orbit,
this tilt and a positive total weight as in \ref{fdip-010R}.
A globally sign-invariant
weight also assigns equal zero-field totals to the two values at
#{x}, by flipping all signs. Tilting only #{x} gives the same
hyperbolic tangent there. With sources at several sites,
\ref{fdip-010Q} gives a product scalar marginal in the gauge
model. In the [global hopping weight](fdip-00ZW) neighboring
scalars can remain coupled. The calculation in \ref{fdip-0101}
applies its site field at all #{9} sites.}

\p{For the isolated two-point integral, choose the positive unit
vector on the real line and natural parameter #{\eta u}.
The coordinate mean of its exponentially tilted unit-sphere
probability is formalized in
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.integral_inner_vonMisesFisherNaturalMeasure_of_finrank_eq_one}.}
